{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794685918,"nonce":"+jk2ezBzXqbpaCmcFnEuiQuAlTw=","message":"Measured wrongly at first: the harness settles for up to 10 s before its first frame, so the finite CSS animation in my first probe had finished and every frame was the end state. The numbers above are those of a settled frame against native text at the wrong scales. Redone with a repeating animation, read from the frames that are mid-animation, with the scale taken from the width of the label's ink.\n\nMid-animation, unfaded label, against text laid out at the scale it has: 0.81 to 0.91 (mean 0.86) before, on the vector, CPU rows and node table paths alike. Cause, from enlarged frames and a sweep of settings: the glyphs were rasterized at the scale rounded up to a quarter and placed at fractional pixels, and a row then moved them by a fractional offset each frame. Rounding alone did not help; the quad has to land on a whole pixel at a size close to 1:1.\n\nNow: 0.95 to 0.98 on all three paths, faded and unfaded, and for a spring-driven zoom 0.95 to 0.99. Changes: the raster size follows the current scale; glyphs are put on whole device pixels while their node scales (CPU twin in node_table::resolve_primitive and the table shader); text that only moves keeps its smooth placement. Desktop cost is within noise (60 text cards 0.21 to 0.25 cores against 0.22 to 0.23, 200 cards 0.39 to 0.41 against 0.39). The size is moved to the glyph atlas's half-pixel step while the scale changes, which keeps the paths' bitmaps identical; the atlas key is 566784b.","files":null}]}